#{[17592186375719 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375719 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375719 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d954d438-acb2-5e8f-94fb-1b1426a9576e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386445 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386445 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109666" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386445 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386445 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +1)^{3}(\\lambda -3)^{2}(\\lambda +2)^{4},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386445 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fdcb3137-9f9a-53d4-9695-935580d7ee98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386445 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +1)^{3}(\\lambda -3)^{2}(\\lambda +2)^{4},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =-1$ has multiplicity $3.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is $3.$\n\nHowever, there are only $2$ eigenvectors corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1$ is $3-2=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386446 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386446 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921863864